  "paragraphs": [
    {
      "id": "yeats-collected-poems-s0412-p00858",
      "source_en": "of the Grand Master of the Templars seems to me fit symbol for those who\nlabour from hatred, and so for sterility in various kinds. 41 It is said to have been\nincorporated in the ritual of certain Masonic societies of the eighteenth century,\nand to have fed class-hatred.\n I suppose that I must have put hawks into the fourth stanza because I have\na ring with a hawk and a butterfly upon it, to symbolize the straight road of\nlogic, and so of mechanism, and the crooked road of intuition: 'For wisdom is\na butterfly and not a gloomy bird of prey.' 42 - 1928.",
